Load Balancer Lesson 2: Internal Architecture
What is inside a Load Balancer?
Most developers imagine a load balancer as:
Request comes → choose server → forward request
Reality is much more complex.
A production load balancer like:
•	NGINX
•	HAProxy
•	Envoy
•	AWS ALB
contains multiple subsystems working together.
Think of a load balancer as a small operating system specialized for network traffic.
The major components are:
1.	Listener
2.	Connection Manager
3.	Routing Engine
4.	Health Checker
5.	Load Balancing Algorithm Engine
6.	SSL/TLS Engine
7.	Metrics & Monitoring
8.	Configuration Manager
________________________________________
1. Listener
Problem
How does the load balancer know a new request arrived?
It needs a component constantly listening on a network port.
Example:
Port 80
Port 443
When a packet arrives:
Client
↓
Port 443
↓
Load Balancer
The listener accepts the connection.
________________________________________
Operating System Level
Internally:
Java
ServerSocket serverSocket = new ServerSocket(443);
Linux does something similar using:
socket()
bind()
listen()
accept()
________________________________________
Bottleneck
Suppose:
100,000 connections/sec
Single-threaded listener becomes slow.
Modern load balancers use:
epoll (Linux)
kqueue (BSD)
IOCP (Windows)
for efficient event-driven processing.
________________________________________
2. Connection Manager
Problem
After accepting the connection, what next?
The load balancer must maintain information about active connections.
Example:
Client A -> Connected
Client B -> Connected
Client C -> Connected
________________________________________
The connection manager stores:
•	Client IP
•	Port
•	Connection start time
•	Current state
•	Backend server assigned
________________________________________
Think of it as:
Java
Map<ConnectionId, ConnectionInfo>
________________________________________
Why Important?
Algorithms like:
Least Connections
depend entirely on this information.
Without tracking active connections, the load balancer cannot know:
Server1 = 500 connections
Server2 = 50 connections
________________________________________
3. Routing Engine
This is the brain.
Its job:
Where should this request go?
Consider:
/api/users
/api/orders
/images/logo.png
Maybe they should go to different servers.
________________________________________
Example:
/api/users -> User Service
/api/orders -> Order Service
/images -> CDN Cluster
The routing engine decides.
________________________________________
NGINX example:
Nginx Config
location /users {
}
Internally this becomes routing rules.
________________________________________
4. Load Balancing Engine
Now assume we have:
Server1
Server2
Server3
The routing engine decided:
User Service Cluster
Now which server?
The load balancing engine applies an algorithm.
Possible algorithms:
•	Round Robin
•	Weighted Round Robin
•	Least Connections
•	Least Latency
•	Consistent Hashing
We'll study each separately.
________________________________________
Internally:
Java
Server chooseServer() {
}
Everything depends on this decision.
________________________________________
5. Health Checker
A huge production component.
Problem
Suppose:
Server1 ✅
Server2 ❌
Server3 ✅
How does the load balancer know Server2 died?
________________________________________
It runs periodic checks.
Example every:
5 seconds
send:
HTTP
GET /health
Expected:
HTTP
200 OK
If response fails repeatedly:
Server marked unhealthy and removed from the routing pool.
________________________________________
Internal structure:
Java
class ServerState {
boolean healthy;
int consecutiveFailures;
}
________________________________________
Why Consecutive Failures?
Single failure does not necessarily mean server crash.
Maybe:
•	temporary network issue
•	GC pause
•	packet loss
Therefore most load balancers require:
3 failures
or
5 failures
before removing a server.
________________________________________
6. SSL/TLS Engine
This is one of the heaviest components.
When user opens:
https://amazon.com
TLS handshake occurs.
Cryptography is expensive.
Load balancers often terminate TLS.
Meaning:
Client <-> LB
HTTPS
and
LB <-> Backend
either HTTP or HTTPS.
________________________________________
Benefits:
•	backend CPUs freed
•	centralized certificates
•	easier management
________________________________________
AWS ALB works this way.
________________________________________
7. Metrics Engine
Production systems run on metrics.
The load balancer continuously records:
•	RPS
•	latency
•	active connections
•	backend failures
•	bytes transferred
________________________________________
Metrics example:
Current Connections: 15,000
Requests/sec: 50,000
Average Latency: 12 ms
________________________________________
Without metrics:
Engineers are blind.
________________________________________
8. Configuration Manager
Suppose new server added:
Server4
The load balancer must update configuration.
________________________________________
The configuration manager controls:
•	routing rules
•	health check rules
•	TLS certificates
•	balancing algorithms
________________________________________
In Kubernetes:
Ingress Controller
plays this role dynamically.
________________________________________
Complete Request Journey
Let's trace one request.
User sends:
